@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
' '   description:  Same bug-fix process shown with role swimlanes; the flat variant is described in a note.
'   usecase:      Contrast swimlane partitions vs partition-less activities; explain when to use each.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       software-process
' ============================================================================
title Example Activity Variant - Swimlanes vs Flat

' A single process can be drawn two ways:
'
'   (A) SWIMLANES  — actions partitioned into columns by responsible ROLE.
'   (B) FLAT       — the same actions in one un-partitioned flow.
'
' The control-flow semantics are IDENTICAL. Partitions are purely a
' presentation device that surfaces WHO does each step.
'
' WHEN TO USE SWIMLANES:
'   - Accountability or hand-offs between people/teams are the point.
'   - Business processes where "which role" matters (approvals, escalations).
'   - Onboarding / SOP diagrams for cross-functional work.
'
' WHEN TO USE FLAT:
'   - The algorithm/logic is the focus, not who performs it.
'   - Technical pipelines, data transforms, stateless services.
'   - When the diagram is already dense and lanes would only add noise.
'
' BELOW: the SWIMLANE version is rendered. The FLAT equivalent is described
' in the closing note so you can see both representations of one process.

|Reporter|
start
:File bug report;

|Triage|
:Reproduce issue;

if (Reproducible?) then (yes)
  |Developer|
  :Implement fix;
  |QA|
  :Verify fix;
else (no)
  |Triage|
  :Close as cannot-reproduce;
endif

|Reporter|
:Confirm resolved;
stop

note over |Reporter|, |QA|
  <<flat variant>>
  The SAME process without partitions reads top-to-bottom:
    start -> File bug -> Reproduce ->
    (if Reproducible?) yes: Implement fix -> Verify
                     no:  Close as cannot-reproduce ->
    Confirm resolved -> stop
  The actions and control flow are unchanged; only the role columns vanish.
end note
@enduml
